# Vulkan precompiled SPIR-V blobs (M4-13-T16) — same LFS-free contract.
# Embedded via include_bytes! in crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/src/spirv.rs
# (SHA-256-pinned there and in SHA256SUMS); regenerated ONLY by
# scripts/compile-vulkan-shaders.sh --update with the toolchain pinned in
# kernels/precompiled/PROVENANCE.
crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/kernels/precompiled/*.spv -filter -diff -merge

# Shader sources whose BYTES are SHA-256-pinned must check out identically on
# every platform. Both are hashed as text-through-bytes:
#   *.wgsl  -> include_str! + expected_sha256_hex in vokra-backend-webgpu/src/wgsl.rs
#   *.comp  -> per-source hashes in vokra-backend-vulkan/kernels/precompiled/PROVENANCE
# Without an eol pin, a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true by default, as on
# the GitHub windows-latest runners) rewrites LF to CRLF and the drift gate
# fires on a file nobody edited — observed as `WGSL source drift for copy_f32`
# in the first Windows CI run of this branch. `eol=lf` (not `-text`) keeps them
# diffable and mergeable as the source files they are.
*.wgsl text eol=lf
*.comp text eol=lf
tools/parity/fixtures/*.csv text eol=lf

# Same reason, one level up: every TEXT file under tests/parity/ is either
# hashed into a manifest or IS the manifest, so a CRLF checkout breaks the
# fixture-drift gates. Fixing these one extension at a time is whack-a-mole
# (the first Windows CI run caught *.wgsl, the second caught
# csm/context.json), so pin them by extension across the fixture trees.
#
# Deliberately NOT `tests/parity/** text eol=lf`: that would set `text` on
# the *binary* fixtures too (*.f32, *.u32, *.bin, *.i16le, *.i64, *.wav) and
# git would then rewrite byte pairs that happen to look like line endings —
# silently corrupting float data on a Windows checkout. The `-filter -diff
# -merge` rules above do NOT prevent eol conversion, so the binaries are
# listed explicitly as `-text` below instead of relying on the NUL-byte
# heuristic.
tests/parity/**/*.txt text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.json text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.py text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.rs text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.md text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.toml text eol=lf
tests/parity/**/*.f32 -text
tests/parity/**/*.u32 -text
tests/parity/**/*.i64 -text
tests/parity/**/*.bin -text
tests/parity/**/*.i16le -text
tests/parity/**/*.wav -text
tests/capi/fixtures/**/*.f32 -text
tests/capi/fixtures/**/*.txt text eol=lf
# The Vulkan hash ledgers themselves: SHA256SUMS is compared line-by-line by
# scripts/compile-vulkan-shaders.sh --check and PROVENANCE carries the
# per-source hashes, so both want stable bytes for the same reason.
crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/kernels/precompiled/SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/kernels/precompiled/PROVENANCE text eol=lf

# --- gaps found by scripts/check-fixture-eol-pins.sh --------------------------
# The rules above are keyed on extension, which is why each Windows CI failure
# so far uncovered exactly one more unpinned extension. These four close the
# remaining holes across every byte-hashed file the gate can find.
#
# *.sha256 — the sidecars are themselves parsed byte-wise by `sha256sum -c`
# (e.g. .github/workflows/parity-whisper-real.yml). They are pure ASCII with no
# NUL bytes, so git's binary heuristic does NOT protect them: a CRLF checkout
# turns the recorded name into `jfk-30s.wav\r` and coreutils reports
# "No such file or directory" / FAILED. Note the asymmetry this fixes —
# moshiko_tensor_manifest.json was pinned by the *.json rule while its own
# .sha256 sidecar was not.
*.sha256 text eol=lf

# *.spv — SHA-256 pinned twice over (crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/src/spirv.rs
# `expected_sha256_hex` + the SHA256SUMS ledger) but previously carried only
# `-filter -diff -merge`, which does not stop eol conversion. Their .comp
# sources, SHA256SUMS and PROVENANCE all got explicit pins; the blobs were
# missed and were relying on the NUL-byte heuristic alone.
crates/vokra-backend-vulkan/kernels/precompiled/*.spv -text

# tests/fixtures/ is a sibling of tests/parity/, so the `tests/parity/**/*.wav`
# rule never reached jfk-30s.wav — whose digest is pinned in five manifests
# plus the sidecar above.
tests/fixtures/**/*.wav -text

# No *.gguf rule existed anywhere; tests/parity/silero_vad/silero-vad-v5.gguf
# is loaded byte-wise by the VAD parity fixtures.
tests/parity/**/*.gguf -text

# M5-06 WFST decode parity fixtures. These live under the crate
# (crates/vokra-core/tests/parity/fixtures/m5-06/, the m2-08 precedent), which
# the root-anchored `tests/parity/**` rules above do NOT reach, so pin the
# crate-relative paths explicitly:
#   *.fst      real OpenFST VectorFst<StdArc> binaries read byte-wise by
#              parity_wfst.rs — LFS-free (`-filter -diff -merge`) + never eol
#              converted (`-text`). They contain NUL bytes so git's heuristic
#              would treat them as binary anyway, but the project pins
#              explicitly rather than trust the heuristic.
#   *.expected line-based text reference parsed token-wise — LF-pinned.
crates/vokra-core/tests/parity/fixtures/m5-06/*.fst -filter -diff -merge -text
crates/vokra-core/tests/parity/fixtures/m5-06/*.expected text eol=lf

# Brand assets (assets/branding/, generated by tools/branding/gen_brand.py).
# Not hash-pinned, so the eol gate does not reach them — but the LFS-free
# contract is the same: a `git clone` must produce the actual bytes, because
# the social preview and the avatars are consumed by GitHub/Hugging Face
# directly from the tree. `-text` also stops any future autocrlf pass from
# corrupting the PNG payloads on Windows checkouts.
assets/branding/*.png -filter -diff -merge -text
assets/branding/*.svg text eol=lf
